{"ts":"2026-11-15T00:49:05.765Z","actor":"claude","stage":"factcheck","action":"factcheck.review","details":{"verdict":"pass","notes":"NASA Science Top Moon Questions: no permanent dark side; the far side gets as much sunlight as the near side; at new moon the near side is in night, so the far side is in day. Moon Facts: tidally locked, the far side is the hemisphere never seen from Earth, 'dark side' is misleading. DSCOVR/EPIC: about 1 million miles (I converted to ~1.6 million km); 'fully illuminated' far side crossing Earth, July 2015 (16 July). The title states the truth (no dark side) so the hook does not reinforce the myth. The 'Far side' marker sits on the Moon disc at (0.30, 0.66), checked on the image."},"gitSha":"bf3b9b1"}
